No strokes. The ordinal is a post-classical scholarly device. In his lifetime (and in Greek), Ptolemy Eurgetes was styled as King Ptolemy, son of Ptolemy and Arsinoë, the Brother (Sibling) Gods. Together with his sister and wife Queen Berenice, they were known as Theoì Euergétai, the Benefactor Gods, hence Ptolemaios Euergetēs or Ptolemy Euergetes. 

That’s from the Greek text of the trilingual Decree of Canopus, which was written during his reign. His stylizations in the Egyptian hieroglyphic version also weave fun things like Living Forever and the Beloved of Ptah into his regnal titles.

